<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Washington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Washington was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> planter and soldier who commanded the Continental Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and forced the British surrender at Yorktown in 1781. He presided over the Constitutional Convention of 1787 and served as the first President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1789 to 1797, setting the precedent of two terms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washington.jpg" alt="George Washington" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Stuart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart_Williamstown_Portrait_of_George_Washington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Constitution it drafted was ratified in 1788 and took effect in 1789, with <b>George Washington</b> as the first president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution took effect on 4 March 1789, and <b>George Washington</b> took the oath as the first president in New York on 30 April.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>On the night of 25–26 December <b>George Washington</b>, its commander-in-chief, led a column from Pennsylvania across the icy Delaware River at McConkey's Ferry, in weather that turned from rain to sleet and snow.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers at <b>George Washington</b> University in 2002 connected it to the Israelites' escape from Egypt in Exodus.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li><li><blockquote>Pro-British words were set to it after the Battle of Bunker Hill in June 1775, while American troops added verses mocking the British and praising <b>George Washington</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander Hamilton, who served as <b>George Washington</b>'s aide in the Revolutionary War, married Elizabeth Schuyler at the Schuyler Mansion in Albany, New York.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in George Washington Birthplace National Monument, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Mount Vernon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.19,-76.93&amp;year=1775" aria-label="George Washington Birthplace National Monument on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2635 792 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Washington"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3085 1062h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3085 1062h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.19,-76.93&amp;year=1775">What was here in 1775? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is our true policy to steer clear of permanent alliances with any portion of the foreign world…&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address, 1796 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/washing.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For happily the Government of the United States, which gives to bigotry no sanction, to persecution no assistance, requires only that they who live under its protection should demean themselves as good citizens…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Hebrew Congregation in Newport, 1790 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/To_the_Hebrew_Congregation_in_Newport,_Rhode_Island" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the preservation of the sacred fire of liberty and the destiny of the republican model of government are justly considered, perhaps, as deeply, as finally, staked on the experiment entrusted to the hands of the American people.&#8221;</p><footer>First Inaugural Address, 1789 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/George_Washington%27s_First_Inaugural_Address" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The alternate domination of one faction over another, sharpened by the spirit of revenge, natural to party dissension, which in different ages and countries has perpetrated the most horrid enormities, is itself a frightful despotism.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address, 1796 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/washing.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Of all the dispositions and habits which lead to political prosperity, religion and morality are indispensable supports.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address, 1796 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/washing.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Observe good faith and justice towards all nations; cultivate peace and harmony with all.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address, 1796 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/washing.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
